body:
Currently there is no origin check for accesses to private class members in the windows object, even though accesses to ordinary properties are checked through the overridden JS object internal methods in https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/638. Private class members (e.g. private fields, private methods) can be installed on the windows object via the super-return trick, like this:

```js
class Base {
  constructor(arg) {
    return arg;
  }
}

class ClassWithPrivateField extends Base {
  #field = 1;
  constructor(arg) {
    super(arg);
  }
}

new ClassWithPrivateField(cross_origin_iframe.contentWindow);
```

(See [this](https://github.com/joyeecheung/web-platform-tests/blob/private-class-window/html/browsers/the-window-object/security-window/window-security-private.https.html) for a draft WPT test).

This seems to be an under-specified part of the HTML spec after the private class members became a later addition to JavaScript. From what I can tell it makes sense to update the HTML spec to intercept the operations on private members and perform [IsPlatformObjectSameOrigin](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#isplatformobjectsameorigin-(-o-)) checks on them too, though it seems to need updates from [the ecma262 side](https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-ordinary-object-internal-methods-and-internal-slots) to expose exotic internal methods for [PrivateFieldAdd](https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-privatefieldadd), [PrivateMethodOrAccessorAdd](https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-privatemethodoraccessoradd), [PrivateGet](https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-privateget) and [PrivateSet](https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-privateset) to make the overriding possible first.

cc @syg
